import Cases.Zip2005.Spec.Zip2005.Sending

set_option autoImplicit false

/-!
# Known-answer vectors

ZIP 2005 ships **no test vectors**. These come from
`zip-2005/testdata/orchard_key_components.json`, the upstream Orchard
key-component vectors already committed to this repository, and they are the only
external oracle available for the derivations transcribed here. Ten vectors.

Two of the fields are what make this worth doing. `internal_rivk` pins
`H^rivk_int` -- the second branch of the `rivk` membership conjunct -- and
**`note_qr_rcm` pins `H^{rcm,Orchard}`**, the quantum-recoverable `rcm` that ZIP
2005 exists to introduce. So the ZIP's own new derivation has a real oracle, from
outside this tree, and every byte of the 137-byte `pre_rcm` is checked: get the
domain separator, a field order, an encoding length or the argument order wrong
and these fail.

## Why `g_d` appears as coordinates

The vectors give `default_pk_d` and `default_d`, not `g_d`. Recovering `g_d` from
`d` needs `DiversifyHash^Orchard = GroupHash^ℙ("z.cash:Orchard-gd", d)`, and
`GroupHash` is the one imported primitive not available: `CompElliptic` has the
SSWU map and the isogeny, but the `hash_to_field` front end is BLAKE2b-shaped
work not yet joined up, and `zcash/ironwood` certifies its bases as constant
tables rather than deriving them. See `Zip2005.Imported`.

So `g_d` is recovered *algebraically* instead, from the vectors' own data:
`pk_d = [ivk] g_d`, hence `g_d = [ivk⁻¹] pk_d`. That inverse is taken in
`𝔽_{r_P}` by the generator script, and the affine coordinates are recorded below
-- generated data, on the same footing as the bytecode trees in `Zip2005Asm/`.

The relation `[ivk] g_d = pk_d` is checked **by the generator**, not here.
`Zip2005.smul` is `zcash/ironwood`'s `nsmul`, which is
`CompElliptic.CurveForms.ShortWeierstrass.smul` -- a *linear* `n`-fold addition,
`O(n)`. That is the right shape for a specification and hopeless for a 255-bit
scalar at elaboration time. `CompElliptic` does have a proven `O(log n)`
`binNsmul`, and `zcash/ironwood` a proven windowed Pippenger MSM, but both are
stated over types with an unconditional `AddCommGroup`; `Point Fp`'s
commutativity and associativity are conditional on `Valid`, so neither applies
without first transporting along the `SWPoint` bridge. Worth doing when something
needs it; not worth doing to check ten known-answer rows.

What Lean *does* check here is everything that costs nothing: both points are
on the curve (`gd_onCurve`, `pkd_onCurve` -- one field equation each, which
catches a corrupted coordinate), and every derivation that does not need scalar
multiplication, which is all of them. So the scalar-multiplication step is the
single link in this file resting on the generator, and it is `scripts/gen-vectors.py`
that asserts it -- the same trust story `scripts/check-generated.sh` gives the
transpiled bytecode.

DO NOT EDIT -- regenerate with `scripts/gen-vectors.py`.
-/

namespace Zip2005.Vectors

open Zcash.Circuits (Fp Fq Point)

/-- One upstream Orchard key-component vector. The 32-byte fields are held as the
integers their little-endian encodings denote (`LEOS2IP`), which is how the
derivations consume them; `g_d` and `pk_d` are held as affine coordinates, for the
reason in the module docstring. -/
structure KeyComponents where
  /-- The spending key `sk`. -/
  sk : ℕ
  /-- `ask = H^ask(sk)`. -/
  ask : ℕ
  /-- `ak = Extract_ℙ(ak^ℙ)`. -/
  ak : ℕ
  /-- `nk = H^nk(sk)`. -/
  nk : ℕ
  /-- `rivk_ext = H^rivk(sk)`, the external commit-ivk randomness. -/
  rivk : ℕ
  /-- `H^rivk_int_rivk_ext(ak, nk)`, the internal commit-ivk randomness. -/
  internalRivk : ℕ
  /-- `ivk = Commit^ivk_rivk(ak, nk)`. -/
  ivk : ℕ
  /-- `g_d`, recovered as `[ivk⁻¹] pk_d`. -/
  gdX : ℕ
  gdY : ℕ
  /-- `pk_d`, the diversified transmission key of the default address. -/
  pkdX : ℕ
  pkdY : ℕ
  /-- The note value `v`. -/
  value : ℕ
  /-- `ρ`. -/
  rho : ℕ
  /-- `rseed`. -/
  rseed : ℕ
  /-- `rcm = H^{rcm,Orchard}_rseed(...)` for a V3 (ZIP 2005) note. -/
  qrRcm : ℕ
  deriving Repr

namespace KeyComponents

/-- `g_d` as a point in the imported vocabulary. -/
def gd (v : KeyComponents) : Point Fp := { x := (v.gdX : Fp), y := (v.gdY : Fp) }

/-- `pk_d` as a point in the imported vocabulary. -/
def pkd (v : KeyComponents) : Point Fp := { x := (v.pkdX : Fp), y := (v.pkdY : Fp) }

end KeyComponents

