#guard all.length == 10

/-! ## Conformance

Every check below is over all ten vectors. They run at elaboration time via
`#guard`, which emits no proof term and so adds nothing to the axiom base --
the posture `Zip2005Asm.lean` already takes, and the reason `native_decide` is
not used anywhere in this package.
-/

open Zcash.Circuits (Fp Fq Point pallasB)

/-- The 32-byte little-endian encoding of a recorded field value, as the
derivations consume it. -/
private def enc (n : ℕ) : List UInt8 := i2leosp 256 n

/-! ### The points are on the curve

Cheap, and the thing that catches a corrupted generated coordinate. -/

#guard all.all fun v => decide (v.gd.y ^ 2 = v.gd.x ^ 3 + pallasB)
#guard all.all fun v => decide (v.pkd.y ^ 2 = v.pkd.x ^ 3 + pallasB)

-- `g_d` and `pk_d` must be non-identity: the ZIP types both as `ℙ*`.
#guard all.all fun v => v.gd != (0 : Point Fp) && v.pkd != (0 : Point Fp)

/-! ### § 4.2.3 key components

`H^ask`, `H^nk` and `H^rivk` against the vectors' `ask`, `nk` and `rivk`. This
pins `PRF^expand`'s personalisation and seed order, the three domain separators,
and -- because `ask` and `rivk` reduce mod `r_P` while `nk` reduces mod `q_P` --
that the two field orders are not interchanged. -/

#guard all.all fun v => (hNk (enc v.sk)).val == v.nk
#guard all.all fun v => (hRivk (enc v.sk)).val == v.rivk

/-! #### `ask` and the sign normalisation

The vectors record `ask` **after** § 4.2.3's `ỹ`-bit normalisation, so `H^ask(sk)`
matches it only up to sign. That is exactly what `askNormalised` says, and it is
checkable here without a scalar multiplication:

Pinning *which* sign each vector takes would need `[ask] 𝒢^Orchard`, hence the
linear `nsmul` -- see the module docstring. What is checkable is that the
normalisation is a negation and nothing else, and that it is not vacuous: four of
the ten vectors are genuinely negated, so a transcription that dropped the
normalisation entirely would fail the second check below. -/

#guard all.all fun v =>
  (hAsk (enc v.sk)).val == v.ask || (-(hAsk (enc v.sk))).val == v.ask

/-- The number of vectors whose `ask` is the negated derivation. -/
def negatedCount : ℕ :=
  (all.filter fun v => (hAsk (enc v.sk)).val != v.ask).length

-- The normalisation genuinely fires: it is neither always nor never a negation.
#guard negatedCount == 4

/-! ### The `rivk` membership conjunct

`H^rivk_int_rivk_ext(ak, nk)` is the second of the two values `rivk` may take in
the Recovery Statement. It is seeded by `rivk_ext` -- not by `sk` -- and
separated by `[0x83]`, both of which this pins. -/

#guard all.all fun v =>
  (hRivkInt (enc v.rivk) (v.ak : Fp) (v.nk : Fp)).val == v.internalRivk

/-! ### ZIP 2005's `H^{rcm,Orchard}` -- the payload

This is the derivation the ZIP exists to introduce, and `note_qr_rcm` is an
oracle for it from outside this tree. `ψ` is not in the vectors, so it is
recomputed by `H^{ψ,Orchard}` first; a wrong `ψ` fails the `rcm` check, so both
are pinned at once.

Every byte of the 137-byte `pre_rcm` is exercised: the `[0x0B]` separator, the
compressed encodings of `g_d` and `pk_d` *in that order*, the 64-bit
little-endian value, `ρ̲`, and `I2LEOSP_256(ψ)`. -/

/-- `ψ = H^{ψ,Orchard}_rseed(ρ̲)` for a vector. -/
def psiOf (v : KeyComponents) : Fp := hPsiOrchard (enc v.rseed) (v.rho : Fp)

/-- `rcm = H^{rcm,Orchard}_rseed(g★_d, pk★_d, v, ρ̲, ψ)` for a vector. -/
def rcmOf (v : KeyComponents) : Fq :=
  hRcmOrchard (enc v.rseed) v.gd v.pkd v.value (v.rho : Fp) (psiOf v)

#guard all.all fun v => (rcmOf v).val == v.qrRcm

-- And through `Derive_rcm` at the Ironwood lead byte, which is how § 4.7.3
-- actually reaches `H^rcm`.
#guard all.all fun v =>
  deriveRcmOrchard (enc v.rseed) 0x03 v.gd v.pkd v.value (v.rho : Fp) (psiOf v)
    == some ((v.qrRcm : Fq))

/-! ### Negative checks

A known-answer test only has teeth if plausible mistakes fail it. Each of these
is a specific way the transcription could have been wrong. -/

-- Swapping `g★_d` and `pk★_d` in `pre_rcm` changes the answer.
#guard all.all fun v =>
  hRcmOrchard (enc v.rseed) v.pkd v.gd v.value (v.rho : Fp) (psiOf v) != (v.qrRcm : Fq)

-- Using the ZIP 212 (`0x02`) derivation instead of ZIP 2005's changes the answer:
-- this is exactly the binding weakness the ZIP removes.
#guard all.all fun v =>
  deriveRcmOrchard (enc v.rseed) 0x02 v.gd v.pkd v.value (v.rho : Fp) (psiOf v)
    != some ((v.qrRcm : Fq))

-- `rivk_int` is seeded by `rivk_ext`, not by `sk`.
#guard all.all fun v =>
  (hRivkInt (enc v.sk) (v.ak : Fp) (v.nk : Fp)).val != v.internalRivk

-- `ak` really is `Extract_ℙ(ak^ℙ)`, i.e. an x-coordinate, and `nk` is not:
-- the two are distinct field elements in every vector.
#guard all.all fun v => v.ak != v.nk

end Zip2005.Vectors
